---
import HubLayout from "../../layouts/HubLayout.astro";
import Quiz from "../../components/quiz/Quiz.tsx";
import Faq from "../../components/Faq.astro";
import { pageDates } from "../../page-dates.js";
import { ENGLISH } from "../../lib/english/bank.js";
import { ENGLISH_FAQS } from "../../lib/english/faqs.js";
import { faqSchema, webAppSchema } from "../../lib/schema.js";
import { CHECKED_ENGLISH, src } from "../../lib/data/sources.js";
const d = pageDates("/toefl/");
const faq = ENGLISH_FAQS.toefl;
const sources = ["etsToeflContent", "etsToeflScores", "etsToeflSpec"].map(src);
---
<HubLayout
  topic="toefl"
  title="TOEFL Practice Test: Free Reading and Writing Questions"
  h1="TOEFL practice: reading and writing"
  description="Free TOEFL-style reading and writing practice with an explanation for every choice, plus the current TOEFL iBT format and score scale as published by ETS."
  lead="Text-based practice for the reading and writing skills the TOEFL iBT tests, with the current test format and score scale as ETS publishes them. These are our own practice questions, not official TOEFL items."
  published={d.published}
  updated={d.updated}
  checked={CHECKED_ENGLISH}
  crumbs={[{ name: "Home", path: "/" }, { name: "TOEFL practice", path: "/toefl/" }]}
  facts={[
    { label: "Reading practice", value: "12 questions" },
    { label: "Writing practice", value: "12 questions" },
    { label: "Test length (ETS)", value: "about 2 hours" },
    { label: "Score scale (ETS)", value: "1–6, half-point steps" },
  ]}
  sources={sources}
  extraSchema={[webAppSchema({ name: "TOEFL reading and writing practice", description: "TOEFL-style reading and writing practice", path: "/toefl/" }), faqSchema(faq)]}
>
  <h2>The TOEFL iBT format</h2>
  <p>The table below comes from ETS's TOEFL iBT test content page, which we read on November 2, 2026. ETS gives approximate base times and adds that test time does not include directions and that, because the test adapts, time and the number of items may vary.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Task types</th><th>Items</th><th>Approx. base time</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Reading</td><td>Complete the Words; Read in Daily Life; Read an Academic Passage</td><td>50</td><td>30 min</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Listening</td><td>Listen and Choose a Response; Listen to a Conversation; Listen to an Announcement; Listen to an Academic Talk</td><td>47</td><td>29 min</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Writing</td><td>Build a Sentence; Write an Email; Write for an Academic Discussion</td><td>12</td><td>23 min</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Speaking</td><td>Listen and Repeat; Take an Interview</td><td>11</td><td>8 min</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>In ETS's 2026 test specifications, the twelve Writing items are ten Build a Sentence items, one Write an Email task and one Write for an Academic Discussion task. A Build a Sentence item is worth up to 1 point; the email and the discussion response are worth up to 5 points each.</p>

  <h2>The score scale</h2>
  <p>ETS says that from January 21, 2026 TOEFL iBT score reports use a 1–6 scale in half-point increments, aligned with the Common European Framework of Reference (CEFR). You receive four section scores and an overall score. The overall score is the average of the four sections, rounded to the nearest half band. For a two-year transition period after January 2026, reports also include a comparable overall score on the older 0–120 scale.</p>
  <p>ETS offers the test at an authorized test center and as a Home Edition taken on a computer at home with a live proctor, and says that writing assistance software is not permitted.</p>

  <h2>What this page practices</h2>
  <p>This site has no audio, so we only practice the reading and writing skills. The questions are multiple choice. They train the same abilities (understanding short everyday texts, reading an academic passage, controlling word order, and writing a suitable email or discussion reply), but they do not look like the real on-screen tasks and they are not scored like them. The real writing tasks ask you to type your own response, which a multiple-choice page cannot test.</p>

  <h2 id="practice-set">Reading practice</h2>
  <p>Twelve questions: four on words in a short text, four on everyday notices and messages, and four on short academic passages.</p>
  <div class="not-prose"><Quiz client:visible questions={(ENGLISH as any)["toefl-reading"].items} skill="toefl-reading" /></div>

  <h2>Writing practice</h2>
  <p>Twelve questions: six on word order, three on writing a suitable email sentence, and three on adding to an academic discussion.</p>
  <div class="not-prose"><Quiz client:visible questions={(ENGLISH as any)["toefl-writing"].items} skill="toefl-writing" /></div>

  <h2>How to prepare beyond this page</h2>
  <p>Use this page to warm up, and use ETS's own preparation materials for the real task formats, the listening and speaking sections, and full timed practice. Read the TOEFL iBT specification on the ETS site to see exactly what each task measures.</p>

  <Faq items={faq} />
  <p class="text-muted-foreground text-sm">TOEFL and TOEFL iBT are registered trademarks of ETS. This site is not affiliated with or endorsed by ETS.</p>
</HubLayout>
